{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-37652",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(q)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{q}^{5}-7{q}^{4}+10{q}^{3}-6q & =-{q}^{5}+11{q}^{4}-2{q}^{3}-6q \\\\ 6{q}^{5}-18{q}^{4}+10{q}^{3}-6q & =-2{q}^{3}-6q \\\\ 6{q}^{5}-18{q}^{4}+12{q}^{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $6{q}^{3}.$ We can factor out the $6{q}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{q}^{5}-18{q}^{4}+12{q}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{q}^{3}({q}^{2}-3q+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${q}^{2}-3q+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{q}^{3}({q}^{2}-3q+2) & =0 \\\\ 6{q}^{3}(q-1)(q-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $q=0,$ $q=1,$ and $q=2.$\n\nTherefore, using the zero product rule, we have that the solutions are $0,\\,1$ and $2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
